{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789321406,"nonce":"feK/sQm+/uawufHew6i7NFvsZMo=","title":"Python: a typed list does not widen when a write puts another type in it","message":"xs = [1, 2]\n    xs.append(\"s\")      # CPython: [1, 2, 's']; here: TypeError\n    ys = [0] * n\n    ys[i] = 2.5         # CPython widens; here: TypeError\n\n    def g(zs):\n        zs.append(2.5)\n    g(xs)               # the alias inside g sees the same list\n\nA list literal fixes the element kind (`List(Int)` for `[1, 2]`), and inference never revisits it: writes through `append`, `insert`, `extend`, `+=` and `xs[i] = v` are not joined into the element type, and a list reaching another function's parameter, a field, a global or a return value is the same storage under another name, so widening one name must widen all of them. Converting at a boundary (`to_any`) copies and breaks the aliasing that Python guarantees.\n\nThe fix is unification: every list-producing expression gets an element type variable during inference; assignments, arguments, returns, fields and globals unify the variables of the names they connect; writes join into the variable; a variable is resolved once at the end (mixed kinds settle to `Object`) and the literal is created as that kind, so no conversion is ever needed. The lowering reads the resolved kind by the expression's range.\n\nUntil then a mismatched write is a TypeError (82d40de) rather than silent garbage.","files":null}]}